In the US, students from the wealthiest quartile are 8× more likely to earn a bachelor's degree than those from the poorest quartile — a gap that persists even controlling for academic ability.
Germany abolished university tuition fees in 2014; university enrolment among students from non-university-educated families rose 8% in the following 5 years.
Student debt averaging $37,000 per borrower in the US delays first home purchase by an average of 7 years, reduces retirement savings, and deters career risk-taking — structural barriers to mobility that free education eliminates.
Norway, Finland, and Germany — all with free or near-free higher education — rank in the top 10 globally for social mobility; the US ranks 27th (World Economic Forum, 2020).
